#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int 2dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{2x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{2x} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y) &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x} \\\\\ne^{2x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 2e^{2x} \\cdot y &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{2x}) &= e^{2x} \\cdot 2e^{-2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}\n$$\n\nWe proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int (3/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{3\\ln x} \\\\\n&= e^{\\ln (x^{3})} \\\\\n&= x^3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{3}{x}y) &= x^{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2}} \\\\\nx^{3} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2} \\cdot y &= x\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nand carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{3}) &= x\\cos x \\\\\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{3})dx &= \\int x\\cos xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= \\int x\\cos xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x\n$$\n\nand $v = \\sin x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nyx^{3} &= \\int x\\cos xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= x\\sin x - \\int \\sin xdx \\\\\nyx^{3} &= x\\sin x + \\cos x + C \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x\\sin x+\\cos x}{x^3}+\\frac{C}{x^3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = 9x^2\n$$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int 3x^{2}dx} \\\\\n&= e^{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{x^3}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x^{3}} \\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2}y) &= e^{x^{3}} \\cdot 9x^{2} \\\\\ne^{x^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} + 3x^{2}e^{x^{3}} \\cdot y &= 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{3}}) &= 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$, making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{3}})dx &= \\int 9x^{2}e^{x^{3}}dx \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= \\int 3e^{u}du \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= 3e^{u} + C \\\\\nye^{x^{3}} &= 3e^{x^{3}} + C \\\\\ny &= 3 + Ce^{-x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= 3 + Ce^{-0^{3}} \\\\\n1 &= 3 + C \\\\\nC &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f19168-f0bb-53bf-9956-a7a50c6b83ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085450} {:answer/id #uuid "897d2937-d3bb-5f1d-849c-85706af227a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085451} {:answer/id #uuid "de1ec696-f652-50e2-a2ca-f60ae1ce6225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085452} {:answer/id #uuid "f57e25a5-9568-5240-a324-3f22961eb106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} - (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1ec696-f652-50e2-a2ca-f60ae1ce6225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085452} :answer-field/id #uuid "18274cfc-b655-5f0f-a270-2991bcbf13c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9415bae7-50c8-54d0-83dc-c31d25edcec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085455} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecdf039-5776-5aeb-ba13-590c15e25f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085456} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d29939-8e0d-5470-8a46-7296d8eea87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085457} {:answer/id #uuid "d68c0ed3-905c-539d-a8c8-cb5c3575856b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d29939-8e0d-5470-8a46-7296d8eea87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085457} :answer-field/id #uuid "9abd4e3a-2a51-563b-82f6-79ed2f082bba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bd80c89-2759-5cf8-8ba7-8fb4c49482e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335810" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 4x^{2}, y(1) = 2$\nSelect the correct options below. An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "On the interval $x>0$ containing the initial point, divide by $x$ to obtain $y^{\\prime}+\\frac2x y=4x$. An integrating factor is $I(x)=x^2$. Then $(x^2y)^{\\prime}=4x^3$, so $x^2y=x^4+C$ and $y=x^2+C/x^2$. Since $y(1)=2$, $C=1$. Therefore $y=x^2+\\frac1{x^2}$ for $x>0$."}]}
